# English narration replacing the Vietnamese voice_text (key = "<folder> <scene id>"). Used by make_en_scenes.py
EN = {
"bai-01 s01": "Hello! Today you will learn how to write one strong body paragraph for IELTS Writing Task 2.",
"bai-01 s03": "Idea one: the topic sentence. It states just one clear main idea, and it answers the question directly.",
"bai-01 s05": "Idea two: the explanation. Say why your idea is true. Don't just repeat the topic sentence in different words. Give a cause and a result.",
"bai-01 s07": "Idea three: the example. It must be specific, not vague. It can be a hypothetical situation, but don't invent statistics.",
"bai-01 s09": "Idea four: the link. This sentence connects your paragraph back to the essay question.",
"bai-01 s12": "Quiz one. What is wrong with this sentence: Technology help students? Think about it for a few seconds.",
"bai-01 s13": "Quiz two. Which topic sentence is better, A or B? Choose one, and explain why.",
"bai-01 s14": "Answers. One: helps. Add an s for the third person singular. Two: B, because it has one clear idea. Quiz three: what word does a link sentence often start with? Consequently, or Therefore.",
"bai-01 s15": "Summary: every paragraph has one idea, a real reason, a specific example, and a link back to the question. Now do exercise fourteen in the lesson and submit it for marking.",
"week-01 w01-s01": "Hello! This week you will learn the structure of the IELTS exam, the four marking criteria, and three basic grammar points: tenses, articles, and subject-verb agreement.",
"week-01 w01-s02": "The Academic test has four skills. Listening has four parts and forty questions. Reading has three passages in sixty minutes. Writing has two tasks, and Task 2 counts double compared with Task 1. Speaking has three parts. This is our own summary, so please check the official website before your test day.",
"week-01 w01-s04": "Writing and Speaking are each marked on four criteria. For Writing: task response, coherence and cohesion, lexical resource, and grammatical range and accuracy. For Speaking: fluency and coherence, lexical resource, grammatical range and accuracy, and pronunciation.",
"week-01 w01-s06": "Grammar point one: tenses. The present perfect connects the past with the present, and it goes with for or since. A finished time, like yesterday or last year, needs the past simple.",
"week-01 w01-s08": "Grammar point two: articles. Choose a or an by the first sound of the next word, not by the letter. When you talk about plural or uncountable nouns in general, use no article.",
"week-01 w01-s10": "Grammar point three: subject-verb agreement. Find the head noun and ignore the prepositional phrase. Advice and research are uncountable, so they take a singular verb. One easy mix-up: the number of takes a singular verb, but a number of takes a plural verb.",
"week-01 w01-s12": "Three common mistakes for Vietnamese learners. One: using the present perfect with a finished time such as yesterday. Two: leaving out the article before a job. Three: adding s to uncountable nouns, for example advice or information.",
"week-01 w01-s14": "Mini quiz, three questions. Listen carefully, choose your answers in your head, and then hear the answers in the next scene.",
"week-01 w01-s16": "Answers. One: since, because twenty twenty-two is a point in time. Two: an, because MBA is pronounced em-bee-ay, which starts with a vowel sound. Three: is, because mathematics is uncountable and takes a singular verb.",
"week-01 w01-s17": "Summary: you now know the exam format, the four marking criteria, and three basic grammar points. Next, do the thirty practice questions in the app, then take the entry test. There is no pass mark this week. Just record your score and your first ten mistakes, so you can compare them with week eight.",
"week-03 w03-s01": "Hello! This week you will learn four reading techniques: skimming to get the main idea, scanning to find details, recognising paraphrase, and telling True, False, and Not Given apart.",
"week-03 w03-s02": "First: skimming, which means reading quickly for the main idea. Read the title, the first sentence, and often the last sentence of each paragraph. Don't stop at new words. Then write three to five words next to each paragraph, so you know where to come back to.",
"week-03 w03-s04": "Second: scanning, which means searching for a detail. Decide first what you are looking for: a capitalised name, a number, a date, or a keyword that is hard to change. When you find it, read the sentence or two around it carefully.",
"week-03 w03-s06": "Third: paraphrase. Questions rarely use the exact words from the passage. Expect synonyms, a change of word class, active changed to passive, or an idea expressed in the opposite way. If a question copies the passage word for word, check carefully, because it may be a trap.",
"week-03 w03-s08": "Fourth: True, False, Not Given. True means the passage says the same thing. False means the passage says the opposite. Not Given means there is not enough information to confirm or reject the statement. Watch out for limiting words such as all, most, only, and almost.",
"week-03 w03-s11": "Answers to the two examples. The first is False, because only a few and plenty of have opposite meanings. The second is Not Given, because the passage only says there is a new café and never mentions food. Even if it sounds reasonable, you must not guess.",
"week-03 w03-s12": "Three common mistakes. One: choosing False just because the passage doesn't mention something, when the answer should be Not Given. Two: using outside knowledge instead of relying only on the passage. Three: reading every word carefully from the start and running out of time. Skim first, then scan.",
"week-03 w03-s13": "Mini quiz, three questions. For each one, you hear a short extract from a passage and a statement. Choose True, False, or Not Given. The answers are in the next scene.",
"week-03 w03-s15": "Answers. One: False, because not allowed is the opposite of permitted. Two: Not Given, because the passage only says it was crowded, and doesn't say whether it was the first time the event was held. Three: True, because cost nothing is a paraphrase of enter for free.",
"week-03 w03-s16": "Summary: for each passage, skim for two to three minutes, underline the keywords in the questions, scan to find the location, and then read the answer sentence carefully. Do the ten exercises in the app, then take the weekly test with two reading passages. The target is at least sixty-five percent correct. For every wrong answer, note the reason: vocabulary, a Not Given trap, or running out of time.",
"week-04 w04-s01": "Hello! This week has two focus areas. In Listening, you will practise writing names, numbers, and times correctly, and avoiding correction traps. In Speaking Part 1, you will learn to answer in two to three sentences using the A R E framework.",
"week-04 w04-s02": "Section 1 is an everyday conversation between two people, and you often fill in a form. Section 2 is one person talking about a place or an event, often with multiple-choice questions and a map. In the real exam, you hear the recording only once.",
"week-04 w04-s03": "Names and numbers must be exactly right. Easily confused pairs are thirteen and thirty, and fifteen and fifty. Listen to the stress. Numbers ending in teen are stressed at the end, while numbers ending in ty are stressed at the start.",
"week-04 w04-s07": "Before you listen, look at the words around the gap to predict the type of word you need. After a pound sign comes a number, after a comes a singular noun, and after must comes a base verb. Also underline the word limit, because writing more words than allowed is wrong.",
"week-04 w04-s08": "Three common traps. The correction trap: the speaker changes their mind, so choose the final information. The rejection trap: if they say that date is fully booked, then it is not the answer. The used to, but now trap: when you hear used to followed by but now, choose the current information.",
"week-04 w04-s10": "Now to Speaking Part 1. Each answer is two to three sentences, following the A R E framework. A for answer: reply directly. R for reason: give a reason. E for extra: add an example or an exception. Don't give one-word answers, and don't memorise a long speech either.",
"week-04 w04-s13": "Two common mistakes in Part 1. One: answering just yes or no and then stopping. Two: memorising a long speech, so you sound like you are reading. Use your own real information. Now, let's do a three-question mini quiz.",
"week-04 w04-s16": "Answers. One: eight forty-five, because a quarter to nine means fifteen minutes before nine. Two: the eleventh, because the speaker corrects the information. Three: B, because it has a direct answer, a reason, and an extra idea.",
"week-04 w04-s17": "Summary: when you listen, write exactly what you hear, predict the word type first, and wait for the final information. When you speak in Part 1, answer with A R E, in two to three sentences, using the correct tense. Do the exercises in the app, record yourself answering ten Part 1 questions, and compare them with the checklist. The weekly test has a Listening target of at least sixty percent.",
"week-05 w05-s01": "Hello! This week you will learn to recognise the four Writing Task 2 question types, write an introduction with a paraphrase and a thesis, and write a conclusion without adding new ideas.",
"week-05 w05-s02": "A well-organised Task 2 essay has four paragraphs: an introduction, two body paragraphs, and a conclusion. Write at least two hundred and fifty words. A suggested split of the forty minutes is five minutes planning, thirty minutes writing, and five minutes checking. Each body paragraph still follows the TEEL model from lesson one.",
"week-05 w05-s03": "There are four question types: opinion, discussion, problem-solution, and two-part. Read the final instruction of the question carefully, because it decides your essay structure. Leaving out part of the question is the biggest task response mistake.",
"week-05 w05-s05": "An introduction has two parts. Sentence one paraphrases the question in your own words: use synonyms, change the word class, or change the sentence structure. Sentence two is your thesis, which states your position or how you will answer. Don't copy the question, and don't write a vague opening.",
"week-05 w05-s08": "A conclusion needs only two sentences. Start with a linking phrase such as in conclusion, restate your view in different words from the introduction, and then summarise your reasons or give a recommendation directly connected to your view. If an idea is not in the body paragraphs, don't put it in the conclusion.",
"week-05 w05-s10": "Body paragraphs follow the question type. For discussion, give one view per paragraph, and state your own opinion. For two-part questions, write one paragraph per question, and answer both. For problem-solution, paragraph one describes the problems and paragraph two gives matching solutions.",
"week-05 w05-s12": "Four common mistakes. One: copying the question word for word as your introduction. Two: having no clear thesis. Three: missing part of the question, especially in two-part questions. Four: adding new ideas in the conclusion. Also, don't begin every sentence mechanically with firstly, secondly, or moreover.",
"week-05 w05-s13": "Mini quiz, three questions. Listen to each prompt and decide whether it is an opinion, discussion, problem-solution, or two-part question. The answers are in the next scene.",
"week-05 w05-s15": "Answers. One: opinion, because it asks to what extent do you agree or disagree. Two: problem-solution, because it asks about both the problem and how to solve it. Three: two-part, because it has two separate questions, why this is happening, and whether it is a positive or negative development, so you must answer both.",
"week-05 w05-s16": "Summary: identify the question type, write an introduction with a paraphrase and a thesis, cover every part of the question in the body, and write a conclusion with no new ideas. Do the ten exercises in the app, then write a two-hundred-and-fifty-word essay in forty minutes. After that, mark it yourself on the four criteria and rewrite your weakest body paragraph. Your self-marked score is only an estimate, and it doesn't replace a real exam.",
"week-06 w06-s01": "Hello! This week you will practise three things: matching headings to paragraphs, completing summaries, and learning vocabulary for the environment and health.",
"week-06 w06-s02": "Point one: Matching Headings. A heading sums up the main idea of the whole paragraph, not a small detail. Read the first sentence and the last sentence, and then ask yourself: what is this paragraph doing?",
"week-06 w06-s04": "The most common trap is choosing a heading just because it shares a word with the paragraph. For example, a paragraph mentions boats once, but it is really about people changing their jobs.",
"week-06 w06-s06": "Point two: Summary Completion. Before you look for the answer, predict whether the missing word is a noun, a verb, or an adjective. Then check the word limit and copy the spelling exactly.",
"week-06 w06-s08": "Point three: Yes, No, Not Given asks about the writer's opinion. True, False, Not Given asks about facts. If the writer only reports events and gives no opinion, the answer is Not Given.",
"week-06 w06-s10": "Point four: vocabulary. First, the environment group. Please repeat after me.",
"week-06 w06-s13": "Four common mistakes for Vietnamese learners. One: choosing a heading because it repeats a word. Two: writing two words when the question allows only one. Three: changing the word form when copying from the passage. Four: answering No when the writer only reports events, when it should be Not Given.",
"week-06 w06-s14": "Quiz one: the instruction says ONE WORD ONLY, and you write the river. Is that correct or wrong? Quiz two: the writer only reports that the council banned cars, and gives no opinion. The statement is: the writer believes the ban is wise. Yes, No, or Not Given?",
"week-06 w06-s15": "Quiz three: what do we call a long period with no rain? Drought, or erosion? Choose before you hear the answer.",
"week-06 w06-s16": "Answers. One: wrong, because the river is two words. Two: Not Given, because the writer does not express an opinion. Three: drought, which means a long dry period. Erosion is when soil or rock is worn away.",
"week-06 w06-s17": "Summary: a heading is the main idea of the whole paragraph, a summary needs a predicted word class and the right word limit, and for opinion questions you look for signals such as in my view. Do the week six exercises in the app, time yourself at twenty minutes per passage, and review vocabulary on a schedule of day one, three, seven, and fourteen.",
"week-07 w07-s01": "Hello! This week you will learn to follow a discussion in Listening Section 3, a lecture in Section 4, and to speak for nearly two minutes in Speaking Part 2.",
"week-07 w07-s02": "Point one: Section 3. Two to four people speak. Remember the names and voices from the start, and notice whether each speaker agrees or disagrees with the one before.",
"week-07 w07-s05": "Point two: Section 4 is a lecture, usually with note completion that follows the order of the talk. Stay with the signposting, which are the words that guide you through the lecture.",
"week-07 w07-s07": "Answers in Section 4 are often nouns. Check your spelling and the plural s. Also, the questions often paraphrase what the lecturer says, so listen for the idea, not for identical words.",
"week-07 w07-s08": "Point three: Part 2. You have one minute to prepare. Write only five to eight keywords, not sentences. When you speak, follow this framework: opening, background, highlight, reason, and ending.",
"week-07 w07-s12": "Common mistakes. In Section 3, choosing the first speaker's idea even after the next speaker disagrees. In Section 4, getting lost when the lecturer moves on to a new point. In Part 2, writing full sentences during the one-minute preparation, or speaking for forty seconds and then stopping, or mixing the present tense into a story about the past.",
"week-07 w07-s13": "Quiz one: the phrase on the other hand signals what: an example, a contrast, or a conclusion? Quiz two: when the lecturer says let's turn to, what is the lecture doing? Quiz three: in the one-minute preparation, should you write full sentences or keywords?",
"week-07 w07-s14": "Answers. One: a contrast, which means an opposite idea is coming. Two: moving on to a new section. Three: keywords, because writing full sentences makes you read them out instead of speaking naturally.",
"week-07 w07-s15": "Summary: listen for who agrees and who disagrees, follow the signposting in lectures, and use the five-step framework in Part 2. Do the week seven exercises in the app, record yourself on your phone, listen back, and tick the self-assessment checklist.",
"week-08 w08-s01": "Hello! This week you will write a Task 1 report of over one hundred and fifty words, and use three complex structures: relative clauses, conditionals, and inversion.",
"week-08 w08-s02": "A Task 1 report has four parts: an introduction, an overview, and two detail paragraphs. Please note that every number in this video is made-up illustrative data. In the exam, you may only describe what is actually in the chart.",
"week-08 w08-s04": "Point two: the overview states only the general trend, covers all the main features, and gives no numbers. Start it with the word Overall.",
"week-08 w08-s06": "Point three: prepositions with data. From and to show the starting point and the end point, while by shows the amount of change. The numbers here are illustrative.",
"week-08 w08-s08": "Point four: relative clauses. When you add extra information between two commas, use which or who, and don't use that.",
"week-08 w08-s10": "Point five: conditionals. The second conditional is about unreal situations in the present. The third conditional is about unreal situations in the past, and it uses had plus the past participle, and would have plus the past participle.",
"week-08 w08-s12": "Point six: inversion. After Rarely, Never, or Seldom at the start of a sentence, put the auxiliary verb before the subject. Use it only a few times in your essay.",
"week-08 w08-s14": "Common mistakes. Putting numbers in the overview. Inventing reasons that the chart doesn't give. Writing rose to fifty-five from thirty instead of rose from thirty to fifty-five. Using that after a comma. And forgetting to invert the auxiliary after Rarely.",
"week-08 w08-s15": "Quiz one: the sentence In 2020, Café A reached fifty-five percent. Is it an overview or a detail? Quiz two: fix the mistake in Tourists, that visit the old town, spend more. Quiz three: fix the mistake in Rarely visitors come here.",
"week-08 w08-s16": "Answers. One: detail, because it has a specific number. Two: use who instead of that, because there is a comma and it refers to people. Three: Rarely do visitors come here. Put the auxiliary do before the subject.",
"week-08 w08-s17": "Summary: paraphrase in the introduction, give an overview with no numbers, and write two detail paragraphs that match the chart. Add which clauses, conditionals, and inversion for variety. Do the week eight exercises in the app, write a Task 1 report in twenty minutes, and then compare it with the model answer.",
"week-09 w09-s01": "Hello! This week you will learn to answer Part 3 questions in twenty-five to forty seconds, and practise three pronunciation skills: word stress, linking, and intonation.",
"week-09 w09-s02": "Point one: the O R E C framework. O for opinion: state your view. R for reason: explain why. E for example: give an example. C for contrast: give the other side. Speak about people and society in general, not only about yourself.",
"week-09 w09-s07": "Point two: word stress. With the endings tion, ic, ity, and logy, the stress falls on the syllable just before the ending. Stressing the wrong syllable makes the word hard to recognise.",
"week-09 w09-s10": "Point three: linking. Instead of dropping the final sound, link the final consonant to the vowel of the next word. For example, turn it off sounds like tur-ni-toff. Function words such as to or can are pronounced lightly.",
"week-09 w09-s12": "Point four: intonation. Use a falling tone at the end of statements and Wh-questions. Use a rising tone for Yes or No questions. A clear fall at the end of your main point makes your answer sound confident.",
"week-09 w09-s13": "Common mistakes. Stressing long words wrongly. Dropping final sounds instead of linking them. Saying every word equally loudly, so it sounds like one word at a time. And in Part 3, answering with one sentence and stopping, or talking for too long without a clear point.",
"week-09 w09-s14": "Quiz one: which syllable is stressed in generation: ge, ne, ra, or tion? Quiz two: in the phrase cheap, fast and reliable, does your voice rise on the first items, or on the last? Quiz three: roughly how many seconds should a Part 3 answer last?",
"week-09 w09-s15": "Answers. One: the syllable ra, because of the rule that words ending in tion are stressed on the syllable just before. Two: rise on the early items and fall on the last one. Three: about twenty-five to forty seconds.",
"week-09 w09-s16": "Summary: use the O R E C framework, stress the right syllable, link your sounds, and fall at the end of your main point. Every day, practise shadowing for fifteen minutes with a model answer: listen to the slow version, repeat right after it, and then move to the normal speed. Then do the week nine exercises in the app and record yourself.",
"week-10 w10-s01": "Hello! This week you will learn how to manage your time in Reading and Writing, and how to correct your mistakes in depth after a timed mock test.",
"week-10 w10-s02": "Strategy one: timing in Reading. Our own suggestion is about seventeen, twenty, and twenty-three minutes. Passage three is usually the hardest, so an even split of twenty minutes each can leave you short of time.",
"week-10 w10-s04": "The ninety-second rule: if you have spent that long on a question and still can't find the answer, write a provisional answer, mark it, and move on. In the last two minutes, fill in every blank that remains.",
"week-10 w10-s05": "Strategy two: timing in Writing. About twenty minutes for Task 1 and about forty minutes for Task 2, and in each task leave time for planning and checking. Don't let Task 1 run into the time for Task 2.",
"week-10 w10-s07": "Strategy three: deep error correction with an error log. Sort your old mistakes into groups, and then proofread in passes. In pass one, check only verbs and subjects. In pass two, articles and plural nouns. In pass three, linking words and punctuation.",
"week-10 w10-s11": "Strategy four: after a mock test, mark yourself on the four criteria, and then choose exactly three goals, starting with your lowest criterion. Goals must be measurable. This is only our own estimate, not a real score.",
"week-10 w10-s13": "Common mistakes: splitting Reading evenly into twenty, twenty, and twenty minutes, getting stuck for four or five minutes on a single gap-fill question, and setting vague goals such as improve grammar.",
"week-10 w10-s14": "Mini quiz, question one. If you split your time evenly, twenty, twenty, and twenty minutes across three passages, what is the main risk? Think for a few seconds.",
"week-10 w10-s15": "Question two. You have spent about ninety seconds on a question, and you still haven't found the answer. What should you do?",
"week-10 w10-s16": "Question three. Which sentence joins two independent clauses with only a comma, A or B?",
"week-10 w10-s17": "Answers. One: you may run short of time on passage three, because it is usually the hardest. Two: write a provisional answer, mark it, and move on. Three: A, because it has no linking word after the comma. Sentence B has so, so it is correct.",
"week-10 w10-s18": "Summary: plan your time with clear checkpoints, proofread in passes, and choose three measurable goals. One important note: the mock test is our own shortened version, so the estimated band may be higher than in reality. The app does not guarantee your real exam result.",
"week-11 w11-s01": "Hello! This week you will use your error log to find weak points, recognise four Listening traps, and learn three ways to recover when you are speaking.",
"week-11 w11-s02": "Strategy one: read your error log before you practise. Give every wrong answer a label, such as spelling, plural, correction trap, or over the word limit. The label that appears most often is your first priority.",
"week-11 w11-s03": "Strategy two: four Listening traps. Trap one: when the speaker corrects the information, the answer is the final version. Trap two: when you hear used to or not any more, the thing that changed is often the trap.",
"week-11 w11-s06": "Trap three is spelling and plurals: missing an s can be marked wrong. Trap four is the word limit. If you lose an answer, don't keep waiting. Listen for the next signpost and find your place again.",
"week-11 w11-s08": "Strategy three: three ways to recover when you speak. One: use natural thinking phrases instead of silence. Two: if you forget a word, paraphrase it with other words. Three: if your answer is too short, extend it with a reason and an example.",
"week-11 w11-s11": "Strategy four: record yourself with your phone or computer, because the app doesn't record. Listen once to count the long pauses, and once to hear your word endings. Then re-record just your weakest answer.",
"week-11 w11-s13": "Common mistakes: writing the first number you hear without waiting for the speaker to correct it, dropping the plural s, and answering a Speaking question with one word such as Yes.",
"week-11 w11-s14": "Mini quiz, question one. You hear: Twenty, no, wait, twenty-five. Which number should you write?",
"week-11 w11-s15": "Question two. The instruction says ONE WORD ONLY, but you write two words, even though the meaning is right. Is that correct or not?",
"week-11 w11-s16": "Question three. You forget a word while speaking. Should you stay silent, or paraphrase? The answer is in the next scene.",
"week-11 w11-s17": "Answers. One: twenty-five, because the final information is the correct one. Two: not correct, because you went over the word limit. Three: paraphrase, and don't stay silent for too long.",
"week-11 w11-s18": "Summary: practise the error label you make most, watch out for correction traps, and use the three speaking recovery methods. Remember that the mock test is our own shortened version, so the estimated band may be higher than in reality. The app does not guarantee your real exam result.",
"week-12 w12-s01": "Hello! In this final week, you will plan your exam day, review your personal mistakes, and learn how to read your mock results sensibly.",
"week-12 w12-s02": "Strategy one: for test-room rules, trust only official sources. This app does not list those rules. Read your registration confirmation carefully, check the test organiser's website, and ask your test centre if anything is unclear.",
"week-12 w12-s04": "The final week is for consolidating, not for learning new material. Review your vocabulary and your personal error list, and take a mock test in your real exam time slot. The day before the exam, do only a light review, prepare what your confirmation letter asks for, check your route, and get enough sleep.",
"week-12 w12-s05": "Strategy two: Listening and Reading. Listening takes about thirty minutes, with forty questions: use the reading time to look ahead, and circle the word limits. Reading takes sixty minutes: about seventeen, twenty, and twenty-three minutes, and in the last two minutes fill in every blank.",
"week-12 w12-s07": "Strategy three: Writing, sixty minutes. If you do Task 1 first, aim to finish it by minute twenty, and start checking Task 2 at minute fifty-five. Choose an order you have practised, and don't change it at the last minute.",
"week-12 w12-s10": "Strategy four: Speaking takes about eleven to fourteen minutes. Speak naturally, extend your answers with reasons and examples, and don't memorise answers. If you didn't hear a question clearly, politely ask the examiner to repeat it. That is better than guessing the wrong question.",
"week-12 w12-s12": "How to read your mock results. The Listening and Reading bands are based on the app's own conversion table. The Writing band uses the formula Task 1 plus two times Task 2, divided by three, rounded down to the nearest half band. This is an estimate, not a real score. For any skill below seven point zero, add two to four more weeks.",
"week-12 w12-s13": "Common mistakes: believing rumours about test-room rules, studying new material late the night before, leaving blanks when time runs out, and writing Task 1 with no overview.",
"week-12 w12-s14": "Mini quiz, question one. Where should you check the test-room rules, to be sure the information is reliable?",
"week-12 w12-s15": "Question two. In Reading, you have two minutes left and four blanks. What should you do?",
"week-12 w12-s16": "Question three. In Speaking, you didn't hear the question clearly. What should you say? Try saying it in English before you hear the answer.",
"week-12 w12-s17": "Answers. One: your confirmation letter, the official website, and your test centre. Two: fill in all four with the most reasonable options. Three: politely ask for the question again, for example: Could you repeat the question, please?",
"week-12 w12-s18": "Summary: rely on official sources, consolidate in the final week, keep to your time checkpoints for each skill, and read your band as an estimate. One important reminder: the mock test is our own shortened version, so the estimated band may be higher than in reality. The app does not guarantee your real exam result. Good luck on your test!",
}
